1
00:00:00,325 --> 00:00:02,000
Would you call a human lineage

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,338
that survived for more than

3
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:05,300
a million years a failure?

4
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:08,038
Homo erectus had an extraordinarily

5
00:00:08,038 --> 00:00:10,100
long history and spread across

6
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:12,462
a vast geographical area.

7
00:00:12,462 --> 00:00:14,075
The details of which fossils

8
00:00:14,075 --> 00:00:15,925
belong together are debated,

9
00:00:15,925 --> 00:00:17,888
so its exact range depends

10
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:19,975
partly on classification.

11
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:21,850
But the scale of its survival

12
00:00:21,850 --> 00:00:23,650
is not in doubt: well over

13
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:25,100
a million years.

14
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:27,512
That matters because we often

15
00:00:27,512 --> 00:00:29,650
tell human evolution backward,

16
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:31,450
as if every earlier branch

17
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:33,425
was only a rough draft of us.

18
00:00:34,025 --> 00:00:35,475
A lineage can be successful

19
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:36,838
in its own environments for

20
00:00:36,838 --> 00:00:38,825
a very long time and still

21
00:00:38,825 --> 00:00:40,500
eventually disappear.

22
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:42,450
Its absence today does not

23
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:43,762
erase the generations that

24
00:00:43,762 --> 00:00:46,175
found food, raised children,

25
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:48,400
and passed on useful skills.

26
00:00:49,025 --> 00:00:51,000
Survival is a history, not

27
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,612
just the name left on the final

28
00:00:52,612 --> 00:00:54,625
page. We happen to be reading

29
00:00:54,625 --> 00:00:56,400
from the branch that remains.
